 Our work is organized around three contributions:
 \begin{itemize}
 
-\item \textbf{GATE: handoff through distinct position and orientation references.} We introduce a task-conditioned geometric handoff formulation that assigns position and orientation to separate reference roles. GATE realizes this formulation through semantic reference selection and closed-loop geometric control, without additional transition-policy training. 
+\item \textbf{GATE: geometric alignment for handoff.} We address navigation-to-manipulation handoff failure with object-aware geometry relations, closed-loop base adjustment, and readiness checks, without additional training or special annotations.
 
 \item \textbf{Agentic humanoid loco-manipulation.} We develop an agentic loco-manipulation system that coordinates navigation, alignment, and humanoid manipulation. The system exhibits robust generalization across diverse workspaces and tasks in real-world settings.
 
-\item \textbf{Long-horizon execution and transfer evaluation.} We evaluate the agentic system on real-world humanoid tasks, improve the overall success rate from from 35.0\% to 82.5\%, and we examine the applicability of GATE on the OVMM benchmark. These experiments assess the geometric handoff principle across different embodiments and downstream manipulation policies.
+\item \textbf{Long-horizon execution and transfer evaluation.} We evaluated the agentic system on real-world humanoid tasks and examine the applicability of GATE in OVMM benchmark. These experiments test the methods across different embodiments and downstream manipulation polices. 
 \end{itemize}
 
